<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  App Sandbox for the Mac App Store build (tools/build-mas.sh).

  NOT used by build-release.sh. The Developer ID build stays unsandboxed on
  purpose: it is the one that has to keep working for people downloading a zip,
  and the sandbox is exactly the variable being tested here.

  Each key below is here because a specific call needs it. Anything that cannot
  be traced to a call site does not belong in this file — a sandbox is only
  worth having if it is the smallest one that works.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- app/main/dictionaries.js resolves GitHub release URLs and downloads the
       freely-licensed dictionaries. Outbound only; the app serves nothing. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- app/main/ipc.js opens a file dialog so a dictionary the user already owns
       can be imported. Read-only: the app copies the zip into its own container
       and never writes back to wherever it came from. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- com.apple.security.application-groups is NOT here because it cannot be:
       its value is "TEAMID.bundle.id" and there is no Team ID yet. Electron's
       MAS guide lists it as required when signing by hand, and Chromium's
       helper processes use the shared group container to reach the main
       process — without it a sandboxed Electron app does not run.
       build-mas.sh injects it when TEAM_ID is set, and says so loudly when it
       is not. Do not hardcode a value here; a stale Team ID would fail in a
       way that looks like a sandbox problem.

       Deliberately absent: com.apple.security.inherit. That one belongs on the
       CHILD (entitlements.mas.inherit.plist), never here. A sandboxed parent
       needs no entitlement to exec a binary inside its own bundle; the child
       simply inherits the sandbox it was spawned from.

       Also absent: any Screen Recording or Accessibility key. Neither is a
       sandbox entitlement — both are TCC grants the user makes at runtime,
       which is why they cannot be requested here and why whether they survive
       sandboxing is the open question this build exists to answer. -->
</dict>
</plist>
